      "provenance": {
        "notes": "Name clash with ornamental salvia (S. nemorosa, own entry, row 64); in Spain and Italy 'salvia' on a shop label is usually this culinary sage. Watering: RHS says drought tolerant once established, in containers keep compost slightly moist but not saturated, winter wet rots the roots; NC State well-drained medium to dry soils, intolerant of wet soils. let_top_dry is the agent's mapping, 7 days a guess. Light: full sun (both). Size: RHS 30 to 90 cm height and spread, NC State up to 60 cm by 60 to 90 cm; 60 by 60 is the agent's middle. Pots at least 30 cm wide and deep with grit (RHS). Short-lived: woody and straggly after six or seven years (RHS). Hardiness: no RHS rating; NC State zones 4 to 8, some coloured cultivars not winter hardy; no temperature stored. Toxicity: ASPCA lists Salvia officinalis non-toxic to dogs, cats and horses; NC State tags agree. Es and it names are machine drafts."
      },
